You are tasked with implementing a collection parser that can handle parsing different types of collections from a list of string values. The `ICollectionParser` interface defines a method `Parse` that takes in the name of the argument and a read-only list of string values and returns the parsed collection as an object.

Your task is to create a concrete implementation of the `ICollectionParser` interface for parsing two specific types of collections: `List<int>` and `Dictionary<string, string>`. The parsing rules for each type are as follows:
- For `List<int>`, the parser should convert each string value to an integer and add it to the list.
- For `Dictionary<string, string>`, the parser should interpret the string values as key-value pairs in the format "key=value" and populate the dictionary accordingly.

You should implement the `CollectionParser` class that provides the required functionality for parsing both types of collections.

Your implementation should adhere to the following requirements:
- Implement the `ICollectionParser` interface with the `Parse` method to handle parsing `List<int>` and `Dictionary<string, string>`.
- The `Parse` method should throw an `ArgumentException` if the input values are invalid or cannot be parsed correctly.
- Ensure that the parsing logic is robust and handles edge cases effectively.

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

internal class CollectionParser : ICollectionParser
{
    public object Parse(string argName, IReadOnlyList<string> values)
    {
        if (typeof(T) == typeof(List<int>))
        {
            List<int> parsedList = new List<int>();
            foreach (var value in values)
            {
                if (int.TryParse(value, out int intValue))
                {
                    parsedList.Add(intValue);
                }
                else
                {
                    throw new ArgumentException($"Invalid value '{value}' for List<int> argument '{argName}'.");
                }
            }
            return parsedList;
        }
        else if (typeof(T) == typeof(Dictionary<string, string>))
        {
            Dictionary<string, string> parsedDictionary = new Dictionary<string, string>();
            foreach (var value in values)
            {
                string[] keyValue = value.Split('=');
                if (keyValue.Length == 2)
                {
                    parsedDictionary[keyValue[0]] = keyValue[1];
                }
                else
                {
                    throw new ArgumentException($"Invalid value '{value}' for Dictionary<string, string> argument '{argName}'.");
                }
            }
            return parsedDictionary;
        }
        else
        {
            throw new ArgumentException($"Unsupported collection type for argument '{argName}'.");
        }
    }
}
```

